prise  to all of us just happened to chose what  could happen in life but we're gonna be  looking at the uninsured see if we can  help them out we're working as part of  that emergency supplemental we'll work  with Congress on programs to help  hospitals as they care for the uninsured  with novel coronavirus now helping them  out sounds like paying for things for  people without insurance  how were we possibly going to afford  that and of course I asked that in a  tiny tongue-in-cheek way but Trump now  says for political reasons to stem the  tide of panic around the virus we're  going to maybe just have to pay for  stuff for people when it comes to this  and meanwhile the Trump administration  is in court trying to make it harder for  uninsured people to get insurance the  Trump administration was ready willing  and eager to repeal Obamacare with  nothing to replace it other than some  vague idea which would by most estimates  lead to 20 or 30 million more people  ending up without insurance and we need  to understand the gravity of what's  going on with this I don't know how many  other progressive shows are talking  about this issue as much as we are  because this is this is not just a  political issue it's life and death for  the nearly hundred thousand people that  have been diagnosed potentially life and  death of course 80% of those cases are  not going to be serious it is an issue  of public health it relates to health  care it relates to a president sowing  disinformation in a way that is actually  potentially dangerous to people's health  understand the gravity of what's going  on with the tests first of all as I  already told you  we are drastically undersupplied with  the virus tests and the reasons why are  numerous and we've already talked about  them number two there are false rumors  spreading that the test itself cost  $3,000 the test does not cost $3,000 to  the end user I mean you don't owe a  hospital 3,000 dollars if you get tested  the CDC has 